title: Pi-Top + AxiDraw = Libre Drawing Machine
type: text/vnd.tiddlywiki

I'm Kristian Bjornard, a graphic designer who runs a boutique practice and teaches graphic design at the [[Maryland Institute College of Art]]. Where I can, I work with Free/Libre/Open Source ([[F/LOSS]]) fonts, software, and [[Public Domain]] imagery: tools and materials anyone can use, copy, modify, and share.

I want to show students and clients that the Apple/[[Adobe]] mono-verse isn't the only way to do this work. I also want the studio equipment we already own to last longer, including a [[vinyl cutter|Vinyl Cutter]] the manufacturer stopped supporting. Those goals led me to F/LOSS, [[GNU/Linux]], [[Raspberry Pis|Raspberry Pi]], and then to the [[Pi-Top]].

In summer 2018 I started using the original Pi-Top. It is a Raspberry Pi built into a laptop-style case, a giant fluorescent green matte plastic wedge. It looks like nothing else in a classroom. It says "I am not the computer you are used to." It's inexpensive, portable, and all-in-one, so I can carry it from classroom to classroom, and it runs my F/LOSS design stack.

I already knew Pis. I had been building my own LCD, Pi, and battery kit, but it was too far, too fast for most of my colleagues to try as anything more than a toy. The Pi-Top gave me a finished version of that idea.

The Pi-Top gave me a computer to design on. I still needed a way to get the work off the screen, past "just hit print."

I'd already been doing that with our department's Roland vinyl cutter. I shoved Sharpies and ballpoint pens into the holder that normally carries the blade and drew a whole bunch of posters that way. It was an [[adhocist|Adhocism]] hack, a tool made from whatever was on hand and pointed at a job nobody designed it for. It was also the first time I could see what [[a new design commons|A New Design Commons]] might look like, one where designers build, modify, and share their own tools. I like pens and pencils and paper, and I want to keep drawing with them while everything else moves onto screens.

The MICA Graphic Design department bought three [[AxiDraws|AxiDraw]], [[pen plotters|Pen Plotter]] that hold a pen and draw a vector file on paper. I adopted one. It draws from [[Inkscape]], a libre vector drawing program, through a plugin you download and install, so it takes a little setup on more than one front, but it's doable.

The AxiDraw and the Pi-Top were a good match. Both sit outside the usual design workflow. The AxiDraw's maker was clear that its software is open source, even though not every part of the system is public, and the Pi-Top runs on a Free/Libre stack. All I had to do was connect them through Inkscape.

I mainly wanted the Pi-Top as a movable control station for the AxiDraw at workshops around the department, and as a design laptop with only the few programs I needed. So I skipped Polaris, the Pi-Top's stock operating system (since replaced by newer versions), and built my own minimal one on [[Raspbian]]. I thought that a smaller install should leave more of the machine's power for the graphics programs themselves, and it did seem to make things run a little better.

I kept the case. The green wedge was sort of the point.

I started with [[Raspbian Lite|Raspbian]], a stripped-down install that boots to a text console, and added the few packages the Pi-Top hardware needs. Then I kept the interface as small as I could. I installed only [[OpenBox]], a window manager. It is a graphical interface at its most minimal: it draws windows you can move and resize, and nothing else. There's no taskbar, no file manager, no icons. I followed [[a forum guide|https://forums.raspberrypi.com/viewtopic.php?p=890408]] for putting a desktop on Raspbian Lite, though I may have used a different one.

The Pi-Top boots to a text terminal. I log in, type `startx`, and land on a totally blank desktop. From there I open a terminal window and launch the programs I need, like Inkscape.

Raspbian (renamed Raspberry Pi OS in May 2020) also comes in a version with just the essentials plus the PIXEL desktop. If I did this again I'd start there, and skip some of the trouble. Still, building the window manager from scratch taught me how the pieces fit, and I got there by fixing missing packages one at a time. For a newer walkthrough of the same idea, see [[this 2021 guide|https://forums.raspberrypi.com/viewtopic.php?t=312104]], which adds a panel and file manager to OpenBox on Raspberry Pi OS Lite.

Okay! The Pi-Top was working, I could get OpenBox to run — but I still had no real design tools! I need to get Inkscape installed and running and then I needed to get the Pi Top and Inkscape to talk to the AxiDraw.

I made sure to run `apt-get update` and then installed Inkscape and all its dependencies from apt-get: `apt-get install inkscape` (you might need to use sudo to run these properly depending on your setup). So, once Inkscape was working, I followed the download instructions from AxiDraw and got the AxiDraw plugins in the right spots for Inkscape to find. A `sudo reboot` later and I had a pen on a 2d drawing robot ready to recreate whatever I could vectorize on the Pi-Top in Inkscape!

Last, I cloned my [[libreFontLibrary|https://github.com/bjornmeansbear/libreFontLibrary]], a git repo of favorite open source and [[libre fonts|The Free Font Movement]], onto the Pi-Top, so everything I might want to set type with is there. It holds about 140 families. See also the [[Free Font Manifesto]].

I had never worked with an install this minimal. I still hit a missing package or a mismatched dependency now and then, Python versions most often. Most of it was easy, and the fix was usually a web search away.

Since getting the AxiDraw working, I've put other programs on the Pi-Top. [[InkCut]], a libre 2D plotter controller, runs the Roland GX-24 vinyl cutter in our department. On a Mac, the only way to drive that cutter in the production studio is Roland's plugin for Adobe Illustrator, and it doesn't work with Illustrator 2025 or 2026. InkCut doesn't need Illustrator at all.

The Pi-Top had a real limit. Most clients didn't want to learn new tools, so they still asked for Adobe formats or listed Adobe programs in their requirements, which made the Pi-Top hard to use for client work. It also couldn't project by default. A Pi is hackable, so fixing that would have made a good project, some kind of graphical output to connect it to a projector. I never built it.

''Update, October 2026.'' I'm building a Raspberry Pi server for the same cutter. Anyone can drop an SVG onto it and have it cut. It isn't a Pi-Top, but it grew out of experiments that began with the Pi-Top in summer 2018 and ran into 2020. The server is running. I'm still fixing the bugs between dropping a file in and the cutter cutting it.

''A note on the AxiDraw.'' In 2024 its maker, Evil Mad Scientist, was acquired by Bantam Tools, and the AxiDraw is discontinued. The AxiDraw's software is [[open source under the GPL|https://github.com/evil-mad/axidraw]]. The successor, the [[NextDraw|https://bantamtools.com/products/bantam-tools-nextdraw-8511]], lists at $949, about double the [[$475|https://www.adafruit.com/product/3509]] the AxiDraw V3 cost, and Bantam's site calls its software proprietary.
